In this project, we try to explore the strengths and limitations of simple Bag-of-Words models or classifying fake and true news. Using Kaggle Fake and True News datasets, cleaned and merged into a single dataframe with normalized text, labels, and unique IDs. To ensure that the model was trained on consistent,  content representative of both categories.
the raw text was transformed into numerical features through TF-IDF, encoding word and n-gram frequency and uniqueness across the corpus. then trained a Logistic Regression classifier on these representations, achieving a lightweight but strong baseline model. Performance was evaluated on a held-out test set, where the model achieved high accuracy and ROC-AUC.  Using feature attribution, we examined which words contributed most to the model's predictions. Real news reports reliably highlighted neutral, institutional language such as "reuters," "said," and governmental or geographic references. In contrast, fake news depended more on sensational and partisan keywords like "video," "just," "hillary,".  
Finally, demo utilities were coded to sample lengthy articles from the test set and print model predictions and top contributors. 
In order for the program to access the data please unzip the true and false files and move the data sets from the sub folders to the primary folder.
